 "id": "2026-10-20-alberta-vote",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "The world's fourth-largest oil reserves just voted on leaving Canada. What happened?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "Alberta voted on Monday. The ballot carried ten questions, but one drew the world's attention. Should Alberta remain a province, or begin the legal process toward a binding separation vote? NPR reports that ballots are counted by hand, so results come slowly."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "Polls before the vote put support for starting that process at around twenty-three percent. Premier Danielle Smith says she wants Alberta to stay, but called that figure too high. Fortune notes that Alberta sits on the fourth-largest oil reserves on Earth."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "Prime Minister Mark Carney called the vote a dangerous bluff. He says the United States is trying to break Canada. Separatist leaders say their movement will outlast a defeat. The vote itself does not split the country."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will Alberta still be part of Canada in ten years? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
